This setup revolves around making camera units from copper_wire, with BatteryTech supplying the raw material into the Pilot Line and finished camera units then moving on to the Logistics Center before serving Ross Associates. Each camera requires 1 unit of copper_wire, so the flow is pretty direct: copper_wire comes in, testing at the Pilot Line converts it into camera units, and those finished goods are positioned for customer fulfillment. The copper_wire supply is sourced on demand, with BatteryTech typically delivering in anywhere from 6.0 weeks to 11.0 weeks at a cost of $373.533 per unit, and supplier invoices are settled between 26.0 weeks and 51.0 weeks after receipt. For that raw material stock, the carrying cost is $5.256 per unit and the shortage penalty is $4.979 per unit, with inventory reviewed every 20 weeks. Production at the Pilot Line uses a testing operation that takes between 7.0 weeks and 25.0 weeks to turn copper_wire into camera units, and the facility manages both camera and copper_wire inventory. On the finished goods side, the business starts with 1418502 units of camera inventory on hand. Finished camera shortages are much more expensive, at $43.801 per unit, and that stock is checked every 1 week. Demand comes from Ross Associates on a fixed 37.0-week ordering rhythm, with each order ranging from 189818.0 units to 1370052.0 units. Customer deliveries take between 17.0 weeks and 28.0 weeks, and the company follows a partial backorder approach, meaning available camera units ship right away while the remaining demand waits until more stock is ready. Each camera sells at $3252.956 per unit, and customer payment is received between 46.0 weeks and 85.0 weeks later. The network itself is simple: BatteryTech feeds the Pilot Line, and the Pilot Line sends camera units to the Logistics Center. The model runs over a 200-week horizon with 1 replication.

