Generate a structured JSON configuration for the following supply chain description. A JSON schema is provided below for reference:


This network revolves around two incoming materials, carbon_fiber and copper_wire, which feed the production of circuit_board and power_module sub-assemblies and ultimately three finished lines: EV_battery, router, and medical_device. The product structures are pretty specific: each circuit_board uses 3 units of carbon_fiber, each power_module consumes 1 unit of copper_wire plus 2 units of carbon_fiber, each EV_battery requires 2 units of copper_wire and 4 units of carbon_fiber, each router uses 1 unit of power_module, and each medical_device is built from 2 units of circuit_board, 3 units of carbon_fiber, and 3 units of power_module. Supply comes from CarbonSupply Co. for carbon_fiber and GlassCore Ltd. for copper_wire, both linked into a network that also includes Main Factory, Plant A, Processing Unit, and the Storage Facility. CarbonSupply Co. delivers carbon_fiber with a lead time somewhere between 15 weeks and 29 weeks, charges $8.99 per unit, offers net 71 days payment, and can supply up to 232071 units per order. On the planning side, carbon_fiber is replenished in periodic lots ranging from 38948.0 units to 85171.0 units, with arrivals scheduled somewhere between 15 weeks and 25 weeks; its shortage penalty is $47.993 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 19 weeks. GlassCore Ltd. supplies copper_wire with lead times between 10 weeks and 29 weeks at $101.777 per unit, and payment is due between 13 days and 82 days after receipt. Copper_wire is also brought in on a regular cycle, typically 63463.0 units with variability of about 5511.0 units, and those replenishments arrive between 7 weeks and 8 weeks; its shortage cost is $8.815 per unit, with reviews every 8 weeks. The physical flow shows carbon_fiber taking 13 weeks to move from CarbonSupply Co. to Main Factory, while copper_wire also goes from GlassCore Ltd. to Main Factory. At Main Factory, the polishing step turns copper_wire and carbon_fiber into EV_battery in exactly 11.0 weeks, using the Injection Molder resource. That machine has capacity for 5 units, runs with a fixed 6.2-week service time, and carries an operating cost of $3.116 per week. Main Factory manages EV_battery, carbon_fiber, and copper_wire inventory, while Plant A manages power_module and router, and Processing Unit handles power_module, circuit_board, carbon_fiber, and medical_device. Plant A converts power_module into router through molding in exactly 7.0 weeks, and Processing Unit performs bonding on circuit_board, carbon_fiber, and power_module to produce medical_device in a steady 8.0 weeks. Inventory positions are very different across the network: circuit_board starts at 2087817 units with a holding cost of $8.567 per unit and a 1 week review cadence, power_module starts at 1215 units with a shortage cost of $7.044 per unit and review every 1 week, EV_battery begins with 2722314 units and carries $6.21 per unit in holding cost plus a $46.392 per unit shortage penalty with review every 1 week, router opens with 852514 units, and medical_device starts with 510349 units. Finished goods from Main Factory, Plant A, and Processing Unit are all routed onward to the Storage Facility. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys EV_battery with orders arriving every 16.0 weeks, requesting between 2916591.0 units and 3369680.0 units, expecting delivery in 7 weeks to 16 weeks, and paying between 14 days and 22 days after delivery; the selling price is $3245.02 per unit, and if inventory is short, the sale is lost. ABB Industrial purchases router with order arrivals ranging from 8.0 weeks to 53.0 weeks, demand centered around 1885119.0 units with variability of 339819.0 units, a reliable 6.0-week customer lead time, and payment terms between 16 days and 20 days; routers sell at $4923.6 per unit, and available stock ships immediately while the remainder is backordered. Ross Associates takes medical_device orders on an exponential pattern averaging 28.0 weeks, with each order fixed at 2243603.0 units, delivery promised between 7 weeks and 21 weeks, and payment due 40 days after receipt. Medical_device sells at $1709.835 per unit, and any unmet demand is simply lost. The scenario is run over a 100-week horizon with 1 replication.

