Given the following supply chain description, generate the corresponding JSON configuration. A reference schema is included below:


This network is built around an Integration Center that turns ceramic_substrate, copper_wire, lithium_cell, and solder_paste into three saleable items: GPU, drone, and CPU, with finished goods then moving on to the Storage Facility. The product structures are fairly layered. A lens_assembly is made from 3 units of ceramic_substrate and 3 units of lithium_cell, while a circuit_board uses 5 units of solder_paste, 4 units of copper_wire, and 1 unit of lithium_cell. On the finished side, each GPU requires 2 units of lithium_cell and 2 units of ceramic_substrate, each drone uses 1 unit of circuit_board, 3 units of solder_paste, 1 unit of copper_wire, and 2 units of lens_assembly, and each CPU consumes 4 units of lens_assembly, 3 units of copper_wire, 3 units of ceramic_substrate, and 4 units of solder_paste. For raw stock, ceramic_substrate is sourced as needed, with a holding cost of $0.392 per unit and a shortage cost of $13.109 per unit, and the position is reviewed every 19 days. Copper_wire follows a min-max style rule, reordering when stock drops below 3161.44 units and building back up to 9544.42 units; it carries a holding cost of $4.725 per unit and is checked every 15 days. Lithium_cell is also managed with a reorder trigger, using 4129.53 units as the reorder point and 11019.48 units as the target level, with a shortage penalty of $43.797 per unit and an 18-day review rhythm. Solder_paste is replenished on an as-needed basis. Inside the plant, lens_assembly starts with 2810054 units on hand, has a holding cost of $0.764 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day. Circuit_board opens at 2966929 units, carries a shortage cost of $46.375 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 day. Finished inventory begins at 1129499 units for GPU, with holding at $2.544 per unit, shortage at $38.599 per unit, and a 1-day review cycle; drone starts at 4494987 units with a holding cost of $5.056 per unit and review every 1 day; CPU begins with 3263888 units in stock. Supplier coverage is split across multiple vendors. BatteryTech provides ceramic_substrate with an exponential lead time averaging 10.0 days, a purchase cost of $250.168 per unit, and payment due 65.0 days after delivery. PrecisionParts is the second ceramic_substrate source, shipping in exactly 3.0 days at $184.044 per unit, with 48.0-day payment terms and capacity capped at 401815 units per order. Copper_wire comes from Memory Star, where lead time ranges from 4.0 days to 11.0 days, cost is $416.673 per unit, payment is due in 81.0 days, and supply is limited to 344222 units per delivery, and also from Process Go., which delivers in a fixed 5.0 days at $271.424 per unit with payment due between 49.0 days and 54.0 days. Lithium_cell is purchased from MegaSupply at $44.412 per unit with a steady 8.0-day lead time, 14.0-day payment terms, and an additional 10.0-day transfer into the Integration Center. CarbonSupply Co. is the alternate lithium_cell source, with delivery somewhere between 7.0 days and 13.0 days, a unit cost of $205.51, payment between 37.0 days and 40.0 days, and a capacity of 344338 units. Solder_paste is supplied by FastChip Corp. with an exponential lead time averaging 12.0 days, a cost of $9.846 per unit, 30.0-day payment, and a further 5.0-day transfer to the site, while NanoMaterials Inc. offers a 7.0-day lead time, $337.612 per unit pricing, and payment terms ranging from 49.0 days to 70.0 days. Production is centered at the Integration Center. The welding step converts lithium_cell and ceramic_substrate into GPU in exactly 22.0 days and uses the Conveyor Belt resource. That Conveyor Belt has capacity for 4 units at a time, a fixed service time of 8.614 days, and operating expense of $19.167 per day. Drone assembly at the same site uses circuit_board, solder_paste, copper_wire, and lens_assembly in an inspection stage that takes between 14.0 days and 25.0 days. CPU production also runs through an inspection step, consuming lens_assembly, copper_wire, ceramic_substrate, and solder_paste, and it takes a constant 15.0 days. The Integration Center is responsible for inventories including lithium_cell, GPU, and ceramic_substrate in one flow, drone, circuit_board, solder_paste, lens_assembly, and copper_wire in another, and ceramic_substrate, CPU, solder_paste, lens_assembly, and copper_wire in a third, before GPUs, drones, and CPUs are all sent onward to the Storage Facility. On the demand side, TechCorp buys GPU, placing orders every 27.0 to 49.0 days, with demand centered around 1628193.0 units and varying by about 414825.0 units. Customer delivery for GPU takes 6.0 days, the selling price is $2520.557 per unit, payment comes 55.0 days later, and any shortfall is handled by shipping available units immediately and backordering the rest. Broadcom Limited purchases drone on a tighter rhythm, with arrivals between 19.0 days and 29.0 days and a fixed order size of 3205316.0 units. Drone orders are delivered in 27.0 days, sold at $2827.148 per unit, and paid in anywhere from 15.0 days to 49.0 days; if inventory is short, available stock is shipped and the remainder is lost. Apple Supply takes CPU orders every 49.0 days, with demand averaging 3069926.0 units and fluctuating by about 841230.0 units. CPU shipments go out in 12.0 days, sell for $2770.164 per unit, and are paid in 22.0 to 55.0 days, with partial shipments allowed and the balance backordered. In structural terms, all 8 suppliers—BatteryTech, Memory Star, MegaSupply, FastChip Corp., PrecisionParts, Process Go., CarbonSupply Co., and NanoMaterials Inc.—feed the 2 facilities, Integration Center and Storage Facility. The model runs in days over a 100-day horizon, with results collected after a 50-day warm-up period and 1 replication.

