 $6.948 per day. It is also subject to breakdowns: uptime averages 185.844 days, and each outage lasts exactly 3.529 days. Finished tablets start with 4532489 units in inventory, with holding cost at $4.275 per unit, shortage cost at $5.411 per unit, and daily review every 1 day. After production, tablets move from the Pilot Line to the Storage Facility. On the demand side, Samsung Electronics places tablet orders every 9.0 days, with order sizes ranging from 967896.0 units to 3739101.0 units. Customer deliveries to Samsung take 2.0 days, the selling price is $2499.044 per unit, and payment is received 53.0 days later; if stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. HP Enterprise orders the same tablet every 59.0 days, with demand between 1132476.0 units and 3186357.0 units. Its delivery lead time varies from 6.0 days to 22.0 days, the selling price is $42.51 per unit, and payment comes in after 31.0 days; in shortages, whatever is available is shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. The network is straightforward: all 3 suppliers feed the 2 facilities, and the model is run over a 200-day horizon with 10 replications.

