This network makes three finished items—FPGA, router, and drone—using aluminum_sheet, microprocessor, and solder_paste as the incoming materials, with Lenovo Group, Samsung Electronics, and Schneider Electric as the end customers. A few pre-assembled parts sit in the middle of the flow: lens_assembly consumes 4 units of microprocessor, power_module uses 4 units of aluminum_sheet and 5 units of solder_paste, and motor_assembly requires 3 units of solder_paste. Final assembly is layered on top of that. Each FPGA uses 2 units of microprocessor plus 3 units of lens_assembly, each router needs 1 unit of solder_paste, 1 unit of motor_assembly, 4 units of microprocessor, and 4 units of aluminum_sheet, and each drone is built from 4 units of power_module, 2 units of aluminum_sheet, 2 units of lens_assembly, and 2 units of motor_assembly. On the raw material side, aluminum_sheet is sourced on an as-needed basis, with a holding cost of $1.24 per unit, a shortage penalty of $41.353 per unit, and stock reviewed every 16 days. Microprocessor inventory follows a min-max style rule: when stock drops below 3505.61 units, it is brought back up to 5278.77 units; it also carries $8.234 per unit in holding cost, $25.361 per unit in shortage cost, and a 19-day review cycle. Solder_paste is also ordered as demand requires, with holding cost at $7.664 per unit, shortage cost at $45.077 per unit, and review every 18 days. For work-in-process stock, lens_assembly starts with 1349867 units on hand, power_module opens with 4565372 units and is monitored every 1 day with a shortage cost of $49.296 per unit, and motor_assembly begins at 3996457 units with a $14.926 per unit shortage cost and a 1-day review. Finished goods are heavily stocked: FPGA starts at 4609276 units with a holding cost of $1.514 per unit and review every 1 day, router begins with 2761742 units, and drone starts with 327104 units with $2.284 per unit holding cost, $33.97 per unit shortage cost, and a 1-day review rhythm. Supply into the system is concentrated on Main Factory. SiliconWorks provides aluminum_sheet with an exponential lead time averaging 3.0 days, charging $31.858 per unit and offering payment terms between 51.0 days and 63.0 days after delivery. MegaSupply is another aluminum_sheet source, slower at an average of 25.0 days, at $459.612 per unit and net 49.0 days. Memory Star also supplies aluminum_sheet, typically arriving in 12.0 to 18.0 days, priced at $128.722 per unit, with payment due 73.0 days after receipt and a maximum order size of 766688 units. Microprocessor supply is split across ChipCo, NanoMaterials Inc., and PolymerSource. ChipCo delivers in 3.0 to 17.0 days, charges $245.493 per unit, gives 18.0 to 88.0 days to pay, and can supply up to 124322 units per order; shipments from ChipCo to Main Factory take an additional 3.0 days to transfer. NanoMaterials Inc. takes exactly 23.0 days, costs $145.018 per unit, has payment terms between 53.0 days and 77.0 days, and also needs 3.0 days for transfer into Main Factory. PolymerSource supplies the same item with an exponential lead time averaging 14.0 days, a unit cost of $250.569, and payment due anywhere from 12.0 days to 48.0 days. For solder_paste, ElectroParts ships in exactly 17.0 days at $336.736 per unit with 32.0-day to 45.0-day payment terms, while BatteryTech is quicker at 14.0 days flat, costs $228.396 per unit, and is paid 29.0 days after delivery. Inside the manufacturing network, Main Factory manages microprocessor, FPGA, and lens_assembly inventory and runs an inspection step that converts microprocessor and lens_assembly into FPGA in a fixed 17.0-day cycle. That operation depends on the Welding Station, which has capacity for 5 units, an exponential service time averaging 8.995 days, and batch processing turned on with batches of 11 units and a maximum wait of 13.31 days. The station is not perfectly reliable either: uptime is exponential with a mean of 31.728 days, downtime is exponential with a mean of 10.935 days, and operating expense runs at $12.069 per day. Plant A manages aluminum_sheet, microprocessor, solder_paste, router, and motor_assembly, and its cutting step turns solder_paste, motor_assembly, microprocessor, and aluminum_sheet into router in 29.0 days. Manufacturing Site holds drone, aluminum_sheet, power_module, motor_assembly, and lens_assembly, and performs bonding to make drone from power_module, aluminum_sheet, lens_assembly, and motor_assembly in 7.0 days. All three finished items then move onward to the Regional Depot: FPGA from Main Factory, router from Plant A, and drone from Manufacturing Site. Customer demand is substantial. Lenovo Group buys FPGA, placing orders every 12.0 to 23.0 days for a constant 3507249.0 units, expects delivery in exactly 26.0 days, pays $2158.059 per unit, and settles invoices 88.0 days later; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. Samsung Electronics purchases router every 26.0 to 36.0 days, each order fixed at 2639447.0 units, with delivery promised in 13.0 to 16.0 days, a selling price of $2839.843 per unit, and payment in 42.0 days; available stock is shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Schneider Electric orders drone every 35.0 to 59.0 days, with demand varying between 2432318.0 units and 4170528.0 units, lead time to the customer between 9.0 days and 12.0 days, a price of $3304.212 per unit, and payment in 26.0 days; any shortfall is backordered until inventory is replenished. The whole setup runs as a 200-day study, with 1 replication and day-based timing throughout.

