de the operation, the Production Center manages circuit_board, epoxy_compound, cooling_unit, solar_panel, and carbon_fiber for a polishing step that turns carbon_fiber, epoxy_compound, cooling_unit, and circuit_board into solar_panel. That step takes between 8.0 weeks and 22.0 weeks and uses the Welding Station. The Welding Station has capacity for 5 units, runs at a steady 2.41 weeks per job, and costs $4.103 per week to operate. The same Production Center also manages lithium_cell, circuit_board, and medical_device for a welding step that converts lithium_cell and circuit_board into medical_device in exactly 27.0 weeks. A third manufacturing flow at the Production Center manages circuit_board, cooling_unit, and camera, where an inspection step transforms cooling_unit and circuit_board into camera in anywhere from 10.0 weeks to 26.0 weeks. The site also has an Assembly Line with capacity of 3 units, service times averaging 4.905 weeks, and an operating cost of $15.743 per week. Starting inventories are quite large: 1356521 units of cooling_unit, 4741146 units of circuit_board, 632582 units of solar_panel, 2283348 units of medical_device, and 3061947 units of camera. Cooling_unit carries a holding cost of $2.245 per unit, a shortage cost of $37.435 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week. Circuit_board is also reviewed every 1 week and has a shortage cost of $48.547 per unit. For finished goods, solar_panel begins with 632582 units on hand, has a shortage cost of $17.388 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week; medical_device starts at 2283348 units, has a holding cost of $1.046 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week; camera opens with 3061947 units, carries a holding cost of $5.351 per unit, a shortage cost of $40.946 per unit, and is checked every 1 week. Customer demand patterns are very different by account. Sony Electronics buys solar_panel, with orders arriving exponentially at an average of 7.0 weeks, demand centered on 242608.0 units with variability of 42823.0 units, and requested delivery somewhere between 8.0 weeks and 22.0 weeks. Solar panels sell to Sony Electronics at $4419.39 per unit, customers pay in 41.0 days, and if inventory is unavailable the sale is lost. Infineon Technologies orders medical_device on a very regular cadence, between 19.0 weeks and 20.0 weeks, with demand around 3479773.0 units and variation of 246250.0 units. Their delivery expectation ranges from 4.0 weeks to 26.0 weeks, the selling price is $4376.389 per unit, payment comes between 11.0 days and 86.0 days, and the service rule is partial fulfillment with the remainder backordered. Schneider Electric purchases camera, with order arrivals averaging 47.0 weeks, a fixed demand of 2790167.0 units, and a firm customer lead time of 8.0 weeks. Cameras are sold at $2745.159 per unit, payment lands between 13.0 days and 67.0 days, and any unmet demand is lost rather than queued. In terms of physical flow, BatteryTech, Process Go., and ElectroParts all feed the Production Center, and the Production Center sends solar_panel, medical_device, and camera onward to the Storage Facility before customer fulfillment.

