This network makes microprocessor, resin, and aluminum_sheet flow from MegaSupply, Process Go., and GlassCore Ltd. into a manufacturing setup built around Fab, Production Center, and a Distribution Center, with the business ultimately selling solar_panel and smartphone products. A lens_assembly is first built up as a sub-component using 5 units of microprocessor, 4 units of aluminum_sheet, and 5 units of resin. That sub-assembly then feeds both finished lines: each solar_panel requires 4 units of lens_assembly plus 4 units of resin, while each smartphone uses 2 units of microprocessor, 1 unit of aluminum_sheet, 2 units of resin, and 4 units of lens_assembly. On the inbound side, microprocessor supply follows a min-max style replenishment rule, reordering when stock drops below 2196.14 units and topping back up to 3449.33 units. MegaSupply provides that item at $117.154 per unit, with a supplier lead time fixed at 11.0 weeks, 72.0 days payment terms, and a maximum order size of 507286 units; the shipment from MegaSupply to Fab takes between 3.0 and 4.0 weeks. Resin is sourced from Process Go. under a threshold policy as well, with a reorder point of 3496.43 units and an order-up-to level of 4530.52 units. Process Go. delivers resin in exactly 7.0 weeks at $224.113 per unit, with payment due 41.0 days after receipt. Resin is reviewed every 1 week and carries a holding cost of $8.078 per unit. Aluminum_sheet is handled more directly, sourced on demand rather than to a stock threshold. GlassCore Ltd. supplies it with a lead time ranging from 16.0 to 21.0 weeks, charges $422.358 per unit, allows up to 876818 units per delivery, and is paid 80.0 days after delivery; material transfer from GlassCore Ltd. to Fab is a consistent 11.0-week move. Aluminum_sheet is also reviewed every 1 week, and shortages cost $0.642 per unit. Inside the network, Fab manages solar_panel, resin, and lens_assembly inventory and runs a testing step that converts lens_assembly and resin into solar_panel, with cycle times varying uniformly between 11.0 and 19.0 weeks. The Production Center manages aluminum_sheet, microprocessor, resin, smartphone, and lens_assembly, and its assembly operation turns microprocessor, aluminum_sheet, resin, and lens_assembly into smartphone in exactly 10.0 weeks. Starting stock is very large for some items, with 3893149 units of lens_assembly on hand, 3654353 units of solar_panel, and 4993808 units of smartphone. Lens_assembly inventory is reviewed every 1 week and held at $1.516 per unit, while solar_panel is also reviewed every 1 week and faces a shortage cost of $28.489 per unit. Finished goods move out through the Distribution Center, with solar_panel shipped there from Fab and smartphone arriving there from Production Center. On the demand side, Sony Electronics buys solar_panel, placing orders somewhere between 31.0 and 45.0 weeks apart, with demand centered around 3409492.0 units and varying by about 182059.0 units. Customer lead time for Sony Electronics is fixed at 13.0 weeks, the selling price is $1762.866 per unit, payment comes in between 35.0 and 89.0 days, and if product is unavailable the sale is lost. ABB Industrial purchases smartphone with order arrivals averaging 9.0 weeks, a fixed demand of 2449227.0 units, an 8.0-week customer lead time, and a selling price of $2298.563 per unit; ABB Industrial also pays in 8.0 days, and this account also runs on a lost-sales basis. The model runs for a horizon of 100 weeks, with the first 30 weeks excluded as warm-up, across 10 replications.

