This network revolves around Plant A, which turns steel_rod and ceramic_substrate into a mix of heat_sink sub-assemblies, smartphone units, tablet units, and industrial_sensor units, with finished goods then moving on to the Distribution Center. The bill of material is pretty material-intensive: each heat_sink uses 3 units of steel_rod and 3 units of ceramic_substrate, each smartphone consumes 4 units of heat_sink, each tablet requires 3 units of steel_rod, 2 units of heat_sink, and 3 units of ceramic_substrate, and each industrial_sensor uses 1 unit of ceramic_substrate. On the inbound side, steel_rod is replenished on a regular schedule every 28 weeks, with order quantities centered around 44764.0 units and varying by about 12722.0 units; its shortage penalty is $9.067 per unit and the stock position is reviewed every 2 weeks. Ceramic_substrate follows a min-max style rule, triggering replenishment when inventory drops below 1823.83 units and restoring it to 2285.7 units, with a shortage cost of $23.452 per unit and a review cadence of 23 weeks. Supply comes from four vendors tied into the same network of PolymerSource, BatteryTech, MegaSupply, Plant A, and the Distribution Center. PolymerSource provides steel_rod with a lead time of 13.0 to 13.0 weeks, effectively 13.0 weeks flat, at $23.002 per unit, offers payment terms of 44.0 to 83.0 days after delivery, and can supply up to 135066 units per order. MegaSupply is the second steel_rod source, shipping in 8.0 to 12.0 weeks at $134.159 per unit with payment due 52.0 to 62.0 days after receipt. BatteryTech supplies ceramic_substrate in exactly 2.0 weeks at $141.106 per unit, with 45.0 to 70.0 days to pay. CarbonSupply Co. is the alternate ceramic_substrate supplier, delivering in 6.0 to 9.0 weeks at $300.46 per unit, on net 62.0 days, with a maximum order size of 267769 units; material from CarbonSupply Co. also takes 1.0 to 8.0 weeks to transfer to Plant A. Inside Plant A, heat_sink and smartphone inventories are managed together for an assembly step that converts heat_sink into smartphone in 3.0 to 19.0 weeks and uses the Assembly Line resource. The same Plant A also runs a fabrication step for tablet, drawing on steel_rod, heat_sink, and ceramic_substrate and finishing in 3.0 to 23.0 weeks, plus a welding step that turns ceramic_substrate into industrial_sensor in 9.0 to 14.0 weeks. The Assembly Line has a capacity of 4 units, a fixed service time of 5.289 weeks, batching turned on at 75 units, and it will wait up to 2.354 weeks to build a batch; running that resource costs $9.627 per week. Inventory starts quite high in this scenario: 3261526 units of heat_sink, 1285202 units of smartphone, 4666425 units of tablet, and 3828775 units of industrial_sensor. Holding costs are $1.449 per unit for heat_sink, $6.573 per unit for smartphone, $1.863 per unit for tablet, and $2.145 per unit for industrial_sensor, with all four reviewed every 1 week; smartphone inventory also carries a shortage cost of $0.794 per unit. Customer demand is split across three big accounts. Sony Electronics buys smartphone, placing orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 20.0 weeks, for a constant 3243687.0 units each time, and expects delivery in 3.0 to 9.0 weeks. Smartphones sell to Sony Electronics at $3397.931 per unit, customer payment comes in 22.0 to 50.0 days, and the fulfillment rule is partial shipment with lost remainder, so available units go out and any shortfall is lost. Dell Technologies orders tablet with exponentially timed arrivals averaging 45.0 weeks; demand is roughly 4624583.0 units with variability of 402467.0 units, delivery is a consistent 19.0 weeks, the selling price is $3670.926 per unit, payment is received 20.0 days later, and any unmet volume is backordered. Ross Associates takes industrial_sensor orders somewhere between every 12.0 and 31.0 weeks, with demand centered on 4050846.0 units and varying by about 258704.0 units; customer lead time ranges from 10.0 to 23.0 weeks, the selling price is $971.059 per unit, payment arrives in 12.0 to 74.0 days, and the business ships available stock immediately while backordering the remainder. The model runs for a 730-week horizon, with results collected after a warm-up period of 50 weeks.

