This network revolves around three finished electronics lines—industrial_sensor, GPU, and FPGA—supported by the raw materials copper_wire and gold_wire, plus the in-process components display_panel and circuit_board. The supplier side includes MetalFab Inc., BatteryTech, and Memory Star, feeding Integration Center, Plant A, Advanced Fab, and a Regional Depot. Copper_wire is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule: when stock falls below 395.25 units, it is brought back up to 963.7 units. Gold_wire, by contrast, is sourced on demand. MetalFab Inc. supplies copper_wire to Integration Center with a fixed 16.0 days lead time, a purchase cost of $139.084 per unit, 30.0-day payment terms, and a delivery limit of 39307 units. Memory Star is the second copper_wire source, shipping to Integration Center in anywhere from 10.0 days to 21.0 days, charging $74.419 per unit, with payment due between 46.0 days and 78.0 days after receipt, and it can supply up to 241194 units per order. BatteryTech provides gold_wire with a consistent 16.0 days supplier lead time, a unit cost of $6.643, and payment terms ranging from 9.0 days to 28.0 days; shipments from BatteryTech to Integration Center also take between 1.0 days and 8.0 days to transfer. On the conversion side, display_panel is built from 3 units of copper_wire, while circuit_board uses 1 unit of gold_wire and 3 units of copper_wire. The finished industrial_sensor requires 2 units of display_panel and 1 unit of copper_wire, GPU consumes 3 units of circuit_board, and FPGA is assembled directly from 4 units of gold_wire. Inventory positions are quite large: display_panel starts at 1507279 units, with a shortage penalty of $9.142 per unit and a 1 day review rhythm; circuit_board opens at 1237407 units, carrying a holding cost of $8.878 per unit, a shortage cost of $29.533 per unit, and the same 1 day review period. Finished goods also begin with substantial stock, including 3665988 units of industrial_sensor, 787554 units of GPU, and 4179958 units of FPGA. GPU inventory adds a holding charge of $1.267 per unit, a shortage cost of $27.81 per unit, and review every 1 day, while FPGA has a shortage cost of $27.576 per unit and is also checked every 1 day. At Integration Center, the testing step turns display_panel and copper_wire into industrial_sensor in a cycle that ranges from 4.0 days to 15.0 days, and this work depends on the Packaging Machine. That machine has capacity for 1 unit at a time, a fixed service time of 5.669 days, and it runs in batches of 73 units with a maximum wait of 9.607 days. It is not perfectly reliable either: uptime is exponentially distributed with a mean of 450.063 days, downtime lasts exactly 18.084 days, and operating the machine costs $9.532 per day. Plant A handles bonding, converting circuit_board into GPU with an operation time between 6.0 days and 15.0 days. Advanced Fab runs the assembly process for FPGA, taking exactly 29.0 days to convert gold_wire into finished output. The Regional Depot serves as the downstream warehouse receiving industrial_sensor from Integration Center, GPU from Plant A, and FPGA from Advanced Fab. On the demand side, Infineon Technologies buys industrial_sensor, placing orders every 20.0 days to 26.0 days and requesting a constant 933571.0 units per order. Customer lead time for that account runs between 9.0 days and 19.0 days, the selling price is $4355.534 per unit, and payment comes in 9.0 days to 80.0 days. If stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Apple Supply purchases GPU with order arrivals averaging 31.0 days, demand varying from 2012539.0 units to 3881972.0 units, customer lead time between 13.0 days and 24.0 days, a selling price of $3205.835 per unit, and receivables collected in 14.0 days to 75.0 days; if GPU is unavailable, the sale is lost. Ross Associates takes FPGA, ordering every 24.0 days to 35.0 days for a fixed 4239366.0 units, with delivery expectations of 3.0 days to 27.0 days, a price of $4129.337 per unit, and payment arriving in 27.0 days to 66.0 days. For shortages there, the business ships whatever is on hand and loses the rest. The model runs over a 500 day horizon with 5 replications.

