This network centers on Advanced Fab, a manufacturing site that works with carbon_fiber and glass_fiber, builds a sub-assembly called power_module, and ultimately turns everything into laptop units, with Regional Depot serving as the downstream warehouse. The material structure is pretty intensive: each power_module uses 3 units of carbon_fiber and 2 units of glass_fiber, while each laptop requires 1 unit of glass_fiber, 3 units of carbon_fiber, and 2 units of power_module. Carbon_fiber is sourced from Process Go. on an as-needed basis, with supply lead times averaging 26.0 weeks, a purchase cost of $88.57 per unit, and payment due somewhere between 47.0 weeks and 87.0 weeks after receipt. Its inventory at Advanced Fab is reviewed every 23 weeks and carries a holding cost of $8.053 per unit. Glass_fiber comes from two suppliers: BatteryTech delivers in exactly 2.0 weeks at $499.992 per unit with net 22.0 weeks payment terms, while GlassCore Ltd. also supplies glass_fiber with an exponential lead time averaging 2.0 weeks, a lower price of $355.226 per unit, and payment due 80.0 weeks after delivery. Internally, glass_fiber is managed with a min-max approach: when stock drops below 1373.97 units, it is replenished up to 2306.11 units. That item is checked every 12 weeks, with a holding cost of $8.705 per unit and a shortage penalty of $34.592 per unit. Advanced Fab keeps 1138307 units of power_module on hand at the start, reviewing that stock every 1 week with a holding cost of $9.479 per unit, and it also begins with 2997639 units of laptop inventory, reviewed every 1 week, with carrying cost of $4.021 per unit and shortage cost of $36.145 per unit. The main production step at Advanced Fab is the coating operation, which takes exactly 28.0 weeks per cycle and consumes glass_fiber, carbon_fiber, and power_module to produce laptop units. The site also uses a Welding Station with capacity for 5 units, an exponential service time averaging 9.596 weeks, and batch processing turned on: jobs are grouped into batches of 87 units, with a maximum wait of 2.606 weeks before a batch is launched. Running that station costs $15.196 per week. Finished laptops move from Advanced Fab to Regional Depot, and customer demand is driven by two major accounts. Sony Electronics places orders for laptop units at intervals ranging from 9.0 weeks to 32.0 weeks, with order sizes between 1880819.0 units and 2515205.0 units. Sony expects delivery in exactly 9.0 weeks, pays $4399.714 per unit, settles invoices 18.0 weeks later, and if stock is unavailable, demand is fully backordered. Siemens Digital orders laptop units every 24.0 weeks, with demand varying from 953641.0 units to 2117631.0 units. Its delivery lead time is exactly 21.0 weeks, the selling price is $4996.628 per unit, and payment comes 38.0 weeks after delivery; under shortages, available units ship immediately and the balance is backordered. The model runs for a 200-week horizon, with results collected after a 50-week warm-up period.

