_device. Router shortages are valued at $47.356 per unit with inventory reviewed every 1 day, while medical_device shortages run at $29.999 per unit and are also checked every 1 day. Production is done at Main Factory. One welding operation converts silicon_wafer, glass_fiber, and lithium_cell into router, using the Conveyor Belt resource and taking between 16.0 days and 20.0 days per cycle. That Conveyor Belt has capacity for 4 units, works with a service time ranging from 5.471 days to 7.317 days, and costs $12.552 per day to operate. A second welding flow at the same Main Factory turns lithium_cell, power_module, silicon_wafer, and glass_fiber into medical_device, with cycle time between 8.0 days and 11.0 days. Main Factory manages lithium_cell, router, glass_fiber, and silicon_wafer in one stream, and silicon_wafer, lithium_cell, glass_fiber, power_module, and medical_device in the other, while the Logistics Center serves as the warehouse receiving both router and medical_device from the plant. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys router with order arrivals averaging 10.0 days, order size somewhere between 2161803.0 units and 2945707.0 units, and customer lead time between 6.0 days and 9.0 days. Any router shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $1269.518 per unit, and cash is collected 81.0 days after delivery. Dell Technologies purchases medical_device with arrivals between 13.0 days and 16.0 days, a fixed demand of 2556383.0 units, and a consistent 29.0-day customer lead time. Those orders are also backordered when stock is unavailable, sold at $4116.276 per unit, and paid 25.0 days later. The model runs for a 200-day horizon, with results measured after a warm-up period of 30 days.

