This network revolves around a Production Center feeding a Logistics Center with three finished goods: FPGA, laptop, and drone. The plant buys in ceramic_substrate, epoxy_compound, plastic_pellet, and aluminum_sheet, and it also builds a fabricated sub-component called cooling_unit before using it in drone production. Each cooling_unit consumes 5 units of plastic_pellet, 2 units of ceramic_substrate, 2 units of aluminum_sheet, and 1 unit of epoxy_compound. The FPGA itself is made from 4 units of aluminum_sheet and 1 unit of ceramic_substrate, the laptop uses 2 units of epoxy_compound and 2 units of plastic_pellet, and each drone requires 2 units of cooling_unit, 4 units of plastic_pellet, and 2 units of aluminum_sheet. For ceramic_substrate, stock is reordered when it drops below 3168.61 units and brought back up to 9016.55 units, with a holding cost of $3.876 per unit and inventory reviewed every 24 days. Epoxy_compound is also run on a min-max policy, with replenishment triggered below 220.43 units and raised to 1396.12 units; it carries $3.078 per unit in holding cost, a shortage penalty of $30.212 per unit, and a 1-day review cycle. Plastic_pellet follows another threshold rule, with a reorder point of 3413.91 units and an order-up-to level of 11176.61 units, plus a holding cost of $7.832 per unit and reviews every 5 days. Aluminum_sheet is sourced on demand, reviewed every 25 days, and shortages cost $8.292 per unit. Inside the plant, cooling_unit starts with 2891376 units on hand, is reviewed every 1 day, and costs $8.025 per unit to hold. Finished goods begin with 4144541 units of FPGA, 795744 units of laptop, and 981313 units of drone. FPGA inventory is reviewed every 1 day and has a shortage cost of $30.46 per unit. Laptop stock is checked every 1 day, with holding cost at $6.578 per unit and shortage cost at $33.603 per unit. Drone inventory is also reviewed every 1 day, and shortages run $28.254 per unit. Supply is split across several vendors. GlassCore Ltd. provides ceramic_substrate with an exponential lead time averaging 4.0 days, at $349.008 per unit, and payment is due 43.0 days after delivery. ChipCo supplies the same ceramic_substrate with an exponential lead time averaging 11.0 days, a unit cost of $171.854, 60.0-day payment terms, and a maximum order size of 327192 units. Epoxy_compound comes from NanoMaterials Inc. in exactly 13.0 days at $158.56 per unit, with payment anywhere from 30.0 to 56.0 days after receipt and capacity capped at 200331 units per order; the transfer from NanoMaterials Inc. to the Production Center takes between 4.0 and 9.0 days. MetalFab Inc. is the second epoxy_compound source, shipping in anywhere from 2.0 to 18.0 days, charging $313.178 per unit, offering payment terms between 18.0 and 39.0 days, and able to supply up to 971997 units per delivery. Plastic_pellet is purchased from BatteryTech in a fixed 6.0 days at $87.95 per unit with net 50.0-day payment and capacity of 476818 units, while PrecisionParts offers the same material with lead times between 7.0 and 23.0 days, a price of $367.604 per unit, payment between 49.0 and 83.0 days, and a limit of 652510 units per order. Aluminum_sheet is sourced from SiliconWorks, with lead time varying uniformly between 13.0 and 25.0 days, a purchase cost of $168.562 per unit, payment due 79.0 days after receipt, and a 7.0-day transfer into the Production Center. Manufacturing is concentrated at the Production Center. One bonding step turns aluminum_sheet and ceramic_substrate into FPGA in between 9.0 and 21.0 days and requires the Conveyor Belt. That Conveyor Belt has capacity for 1 unit at a time, works with service times ranging from 2.751 to 5.888 days, and batches jobs in lots of 13 units, waiting no longer than 15.421 days to fill a batch. It is not perfectly reliable either: uptime is a steady 220.229 days, downtime is exponential with an average of 27.927 days, and it costs $8.163 per day to run. Laptop assembly at the same Production Center converts epoxy_compound and plastic_pellet into finished laptops in 10.0 to 12.0 days. Drone work is handled through an inspection-stage operation that consumes cooling_unit, plastic_pellet, and aluminum_sheet and takes exactly 7.0 days per cycle. Supporting this flow is an Assembly Line with capacity 5 units, exponentially distributed service time averaging 5.759 days, and an operating cost of $10.349 per day. Finished products then move from the Production Center to the Logistics Center for distribution. On the demand side, Siemens Digital buys FPGA with order arrivals averaging every 9.0 days, and each order falls between 971995.0 units and 3833537.0 units. Customer lead time is exactly 17.0 days, the selling price is $1331.472 per unit, payment comes in 36.0 to 39.0 days, and if inventory is unavailable, the sale is lost. Sony Electronics orders laptops anywhere from every 23.0 to 42.0 days, always for 765251.0 units, expects delivery in 19.0 days, pays $2174.002 per unit, settles invoices in 22.0 to 76.0 days, and also follows a lost-sales arrangement. Dell Technologies purchases drones with arrivals between 29.0 and 35.0 days apart, demand ranging from 979555.0 units to 2918123.0 units, a fixed 19.0-day customer lead time, and a selling price of $3880.835 per unit; cash is collected 37.0 to 40.0 days later, and unmet demand is lost here as well. The supplier group of GlassCore Ltd., NanoMaterials Inc., BatteryTech, SiliconWorks, ChipCo, MetalFab Inc., and PrecisionParts all feed the Production Center, while FPGA, laptop, and drone shipments flow onward to the Logistics Center. The scenario is evaluated over a 730-day horizon, with results counted after a 30-day warm-up period, using 1 replication.

