 a 1-day review cycle. On the sales side, Infineon Technologies buys FPGA with orders arriving at a mean of 43.0 days, in quantities between 592313.0 units and 3966777.0 units, and expects delivery in 14.0 to 26.0 days. FPGA sells at $4353.08 per unit, and Infineon pays exactly 46.0 days later; if demand exceeds stock, available units ship first and the remainder is backordered. Ross Associates purchases CPU on a different rhythm, with orders showing up every 9.0 to 21.0 days and demand centered around 4792381.0 units with variability of about 229778.0 units. CPU shipments take 13.0 days flat, the selling price is $2945.885 per unit, and payment comes in 52.0 to 60.0 days; if inventory is unavailable, the sale is lost. Material flows from FastChip Corp. to Plant A for aluminum_sheet and from ElectroParts to Plant A for microprocessor, while both FPGA and CPU move from Plant A to the Fulfillment Hub for distribution. The modeled network includes FastChip Corp., ElectroParts, Plant A, and the Fulfillment Hub, and it is evaluated over a 200-day horizon with results collected after a 30-day warm-up period and 1 replication.

