y is $35.485 per unit, and reviews happen every 25 hours. Epoxy_compound comes in periodic shipments anywhere from 35854.0 to 80435.0 units, arriving every 6.0 hours, and GlassCore Ltd. delivers it in exactly 3.0 hours at $296.264 per unit with 13.0-day payment terms and a maximum order size of 149813 units. Inside the operation, Assembly Hub manages gold_wire, circuit_board, epoxy_compound, sensor_array, resin, industrial_sensor, and carbon_fiber inventory. It begins with 1016172 units of sensor_array on hand, where holding runs $8.59 per unit, shortages cost $39.873 per unit, and review is done every 1 hour. Circuit_board starts at 571523 units, and finished goods inventory opens at 4910288 units of industrial_sensor. Final assembly is performed through a welding step at Assembly Hub, consuming epoxy_compound, carbon_fiber, circuit_board, gold_wire, sensor_array, and resin to produce industrial_sensor on a fixed 1.0-hour cycle, using the Welding Station. That Welding Station has capacity for 3 units, works with service times averaging 5.152 hours, and costs $16.339 per hour to run. An Injection Molder is also available with capacity of 5 units, a service time between 2.753 and 3.55 hours, and an operating cost of $1.623 per hour. On the demand side, Schneider Electric places orders for industrial_sensor every 31.0 hours, always for 1866268.0 units, and expects delivery between 14.0 and 26.0 hours. If stock is tight, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. The selling price to Schneider Electric is $2636.188 per unit, with customer payment arriving between 37.0 and 73.0 days. TechCorp orders the same product every 54.0 hours, with demand varying from 2875671.0 to 4369011.0 units, and it expects fulfillment in exactly 2.0 hours. That account follows a partial-fill lost-sales approach, so available units are shipped and the rest is lost. TechCorp pays $2404.36 per unit, with cash collected between 18.0 and 56.0 days later. The model runs in