converting circuit_board, gold_wire, and steel_rod into smartphone in a cycle that ranges from 8.0 days to 15.0 days. The same Processing Unit also handles circuit_board, gold_wire, steel_rod, solar_panel, and copper_wire for fabrication, producing solar_panel from steel_rod, copper_wire, gold_wire, and circuit_board in anywhere from 17.0 days to 27.0 days. Test Equipment is available as a supporting resource with capacity for 4 units at a time, a service time between 4.859 days and 5.7 days, and an operating cost of $5.226 per day. Starting inventory is 623523 units of circuit_board, 268534 units of smartphone, and 1162300 units of solar_panel. Circuit_board shortages cost $44.736 per unit and are checked every 1 day. Smartphone inventory carries a holding cost of $3.19 per unit, a shortage cost of $27.239 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day. Solar_panel stock has a holding cost of $7.062 per unit and is also reviewed every 1 day. On the demand side, Apple Supply places smartphone orders every 28.0 days, with demand centered around 4011773.0 units and varying by about 1065818.0 units. Customer shipments for smartphone take exactly 26.0 days, the selling price is $4888.432 per unit, and payment comes 47.0 days after delivery. If smartphone demand exceeds available stock, the business ships what it can and loses the remainder. Broadcom Limited buys solar_panel on a fixed 21.0-day cycle, with order volume ranging from 842767.0 units to 3011526.0 units. Solar_panel delivery to that customer takes between 10.0 days and 21.0 days, sells at $979.423 per unit, and is paid in 36.0 to 53.0 days. Here the policy is pure lost sales, so unmet demand is not fulfilled. Structurally, all 3 suppliers feed the Processing Unit with raw materials, and both finished products—smartphone and solar_panel—move from the Processing Unit to the Fulfillment Hub. The model runs for a 500-day horizon with 10 replications.

