This setup revolves around making GPUs through a network that includes GlassCore Ltd., PrecisionParts, CarbonSupply Co., the Assembly Hub, and a Warehouse, with Dell Technologies as the end customer. The raw inputs are plastic_pellet and memory_chip, and the plant also keeps a built-up stock of cooling_unit parts that act like pre-assembled components. Each cooling_unit is made from 4 units of memory_chip and 3 units of plastic_pellet, while each GPU requires 4 units of memory_chip, 4 units of cooling_unit, and 4 units of plastic_pellet. At the Assembly Hub, all 4 inventories—plastic_pellet, memory_chip, cooling_unit, and GPU—are managed together before finished GPUs move on to the Warehouse. Plastic_pellet is sourced on demand, with a holding cost of $2.261 per unit and inventory checks every 7 days, while memory_chip is also replenished as needed. Starting stock is very large, with 4608924 units of cooling_unit already on hand and 4053512 units of GPU in finished goods inventory. GPU inventory carries a holding cost of $4.132 per unit, a shortage penalty of $37.861 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day. Supply for plastic_pellet comes from two vendors: GlassCore Ltd., which delivers with an exponential lead time averaging 7.0 days, charges $144.777 per unit, offers payment terms somewhere between 52.0 days and 66.0 days after delivery, and can supply up to 135589 units per order; and CarbonSupply Co., whose lead time averages 6.0 days, charges $164.305 per unit, gives a fixed 66.0-day payment window, and is capped at 250349 units per delivery. Memory_chip comes from PrecisionParts, with supply arriving in 14.0 to 22.0 days, an added transfer time of exactly 13.0 days to reach the Assembly Hub, a unit cost of $60.13, fixed payment due 85.0 days after receipt, and a maximum order size of 919652 units. Production happens at the manufacturing site called Assembly Hub through a welding step that consumes memory_chip, cooling_unit, and plastic_pellet to produce GPU. That work uses the Assembly Line, which has capacity for 1 unit at a time. Its service time ranges from 0.272 days to 3.275 days, the welding cycle itself runs between 7.0 days and 13.0 days, and the line costs $18.229 per day to operate. Reliability is not perfect: the line stays up for a consistent 177.589 days before failure, and when it does go down, repairs take an exponential average of 11.634 days. On the demand side, Dell Technologies places GPU orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 58.0 days, and each order is for exactly 4071524.0 units. Customer delivery lead time falls between 16.0 days and 24.0 days. The business follows a partial lost-sales approach, meaning available GPU units are shipped and any remaining unmet demand is lost. GPUs sell at $2759.411 per unit, and Dell pays in 9.0 to 20.0 days. The network is simulated over 500 days with 5 replications.

