This network revolves around two finished goods, industrial_sensor and laptop, built from ceramic_substrate and silicon_wafer and handled across SiliconWorks, CarbonSupply Co., BatteryTech, Main Factory, Processing Unit, and the Fulfillment Hub over a 500-week planning horizon with 5 replications. The industrial_sensor uses 3 units of ceramic_substrate and 3 units of silicon_wafer in each unit produced, while each laptop consumes 2 units of silicon_wafer. Ceramic_substrate is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule: when stock falls below 831.12 units, it is brought back up to 1152.45 units. Its stockout penalty is $10.104 per unit, and that position is reviewed every 29 weeks. Supply for that item comes from two sources. SiliconWorks provides ceramic_substrate with an exponential lead time averaging 4 weeks, charges $264.787 per unit, allows payment 77 days after delivery, and can supply up to 927954 units per order. BatteryTech also supplies ceramic_substrate, but on a fixed 28-week lead time at $207.388 per unit with 14-day payment terms; shipments from BatteryTech to Main Factory take between 1 and 6 weeks. Silicon_wafer, by contrast, is replenished on a regular cycle, with batch sizes centered around 14844.0 units and varying by about 2556.0 units, and deliveries arrive every 25 weeks. CarbonSupply Co. is the source for silicon_wafer, with an exponential supplier lead time averaging 2 weeks, a unit cost of $396.535, payment due 24 days after receipt, a delivery cap of 767938 units, and a transfer leg to Main Factory that takes exactly 6 weeks. Production is split between two manufacturing sites. Main Factory manages industrial_sensor, ceramic_substrate, and silicon_wafer, and runs a bonding step that converts ceramic_substrate and silicon_wafer into industrial_sensor in anywhere from 1 to 29 weeks per cycle. Processing Unit manages laptop and silicon_wafer, using a molding step that turns silicon_wafer into laptop in a 13 to 24 week cycle. Finished industrial_sensor output moves from Main Factory to the Fulfillment Hub, and laptops flow from Processing Unit to the Fulfillment Hub. On the equipment side, there is a Welding Station with capacity for 4 units at a time, an exponential service time averaging 2.656 weeks, and operating expense of $8.984 per week. It is fairly reliable but not perfect: it stays up for a consistent 447.543 weeks, then goes down for 3.168 weeks before returning to service. Inventory on the finished goods side is substantial. The business starts with 767723 units of industrial_sensor on hand, with a holding cost of $3.557 per unit and a 1-week review cycle. Laptop inventory opens at 50350 units, carries a shortage penalty of $16.952 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 week. Demand comes from two major customers. Bosch Automotive buys industrial_sensor, placing orders at intervals between 29 and 39 weeks, with demand roughly 4189929.0 units on average and variability of 542678.0 units. Customer deliveries take between 11 and 25 weeks, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $1564.808 per unit, and payment is received 46 days after delivery. Samsung Electronics purchases laptop, with order arrivals averaging 34 weeks, order sizes ranging from 2196091.0 to 2250558.0 units, and customer lead times between 12 and 21 weeks. For laptops, if stock is unavailable the sale is lost, pricing is set at $1519.675 per unit, and customer payment comes 49 days after delivery. Overall, the structure is straightforward: suppliers feed Main Factory with ceramic_substrate and silicon_wafer, Main Factory makes industrial_sensor, Processing Unit makes laptop, and both products are forwarded to the Fulfillment Hub for downstream fulfillment.

