This network centers on a Processing Unit supported by a Warehouse, with inbound supply coming from Memory Star, GlassCore Ltd., ElectroParts, SiliconWorks, PolymerSource, ChipCo, and CarbonSupply Co. The operation handles 4 raw inputs: solder_paste, microprocessor, plastic_pellet, and aluminum_sheet. Those materials feed 1 fabricated component, circuit_board, which is built from 3 units of microprocessor, 5 units of plastic_pellet, 1 unit of solder_paste, and 4 units of aluminum_sheet. Finished output is split across 3 products: EV_battery, which uses 3 units of circuit_board plus 3 units of solder_paste; CPU, which requires 1 unit of microprocessor, 1 unit of plastic_pellet, and 2 units of solder_paste; and GPU, which consumes 4 units of aluminum_sheet. For solder_paste, the business runs a min-max replenishment approach, reordering when stock falls below 1487.22 units and bringing it back up to 2828.38 units. Its carrying cost is $6.718 per unit, the shortage penalty is $12.07 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 3 weeks. Memory Star supplies solder_paste with a lead time somewhere between 9.0 weeks and 26.0 weeks, charges $129.511 per unit, offers 66.0-day payment terms, and can supply up to 701889 units per order; shipments from Memory Star to the Processing Unit take between 7.0 weeks and 11.0 weeks. PolymerSource is the second solder_paste source, with an exponential lead time averaging 23.0 weeks, a price of $321.582 per unit, and payment due 59.0 days after delivery. Microprocessor supply is scheduled in large periodic receipts ranging from 47160.0 units to 95088.0 units, arriving every 12.0 to 24.0 weeks, with inventory reviewed every 23 weeks. Holding that item costs $3.575 per unit and shortages run $7.735 per unit. GlassCore Ltd. delivers microprocessors in 2.0 to 22.0 weeks at $238.28 per unit with net 74.0-day payment, while ChipCo ships in a 14.0 to 22.0 week range at $204.035 per unit, gives 55.0-day payment terms, and has a maximum order size of 953798 units. Plastic_pellet is also replenished periodically, in batches between 35009.0 units and 74034.0 units, with scheduled arrivals somewhere from 6.0 to 15.0 weeks apart. Its holding cost is $7.806 per unit and it is checked every 8 weeks. ElectroParts supplies it with an exponential lead time averaging 1.0 weeks, a unit cost of $306.63, 77.0-day payment terms, and a delivery limit of 460239 units. CarbonSupply Co. offers another source at $153.908 per unit, fixed 15.0-week lead time, payment due between 22.0 days and 69.0 days after receipt, capacity of 967147 units, and transfer time to the plant between 2.0 weeks and 11.0 weeks. Aluminum_sheet is sourced on demand rather than on a fixed schedule, reviewed every 13 weeks, and carries a shortage cost of $36.337 per unit. SiliconWorks provides that material in exactly 26.0 weeks at $402.655 per unit with 15.0-day payment terms. Inside the plant, circuit_board inventory starts at 3393546 units, with a holding cost of $6.93 per unit and a 1-week review cycle. EV_battery finished stock opens at 3501486 units. CPU begins with 3155397 units on hand, and that inventory has a holding cost of $4.016 per unit, a shortage cost of $48.915 per unit, and review every 1 week. GPU starts at 4884623 units. Production itself is split into 3 manufacturing flows, all under the Processing Unit name. In the polishing step, circuit_board and solder_paste are converted into EV_battery, with each run taking between 4.0 weeks and 25.0 weeks and requiring the Robot Arm. That Robot Arm has capacity for 1 unit at a time, a fixed service time of 5.936 weeks, batching turned on at 17 units, a maximum wait of 9.019 weeks, and an operating cost of $9.317 per week. CPU is made in a molding step that combines microprocessor, plastic_pellet, and solder_paste into finished units, and each cycle is a steady 23.0 weeks. GPU goes through a testing step that turns aluminum_sheet into saleable output in 10.0 to 26.0 weeks. The site also has a Quality Scanner with capacity of 5 units, service time ranging from 5.38 weeks to 9.396 weeks, batching enabled at 35 units, maximum batch wait of 10.294 weeks, and operating cost of $9.802 per week. All 3 finished goods—EV_battery, CPU, and GPU—move from the Processing Unit to the Warehouse. On the demand side, Lenovo Group buys EV_battery every 28.0 weeks, with demand fixed at 3874828.0 units per order cycle. Customer lead time is 18.0 weeks, the selling price is $4526.309 per unit, payment arrives 29.0 days later, and available stock is shipped immediately with the remainder backordered. TechCorp orders CPU on an exponential pattern averaging 12.0 weeks, with each demand request set at 4804695.0 units. The promised lead time is 23.0 weeks, the sales price is $3442.337 per unit, payment comes 70.0 days after delivery, and any shortfall is partially fulfilled with the balance backordered. Schneider Electric purchases GPU every 7.0 weeks, with order size varying uniformly between 1238948.0 units and 1372389.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes between 6.0 weeks and 7.0 weeks, the price is $806.414 per unit, payment comes between 47.0 days and 75.0 days, and shortages are handled the same way: ship what is available and backlog the rest. The model runs in weeks over a 730-week horizon, with 5 replications.

