ompound, and completed drone units are then sent onward to the Regional Depot. Finished drone inventory starts at 2466444 units, is reviewed every 1 day, incurs a holding cost of $1.933 per unit, and has a shortage cost of $33.862 per unit. On the demand side, ABB Industrial buys drone units with orders arriving between 26.0 days and 35.0 days, and order quantities ranging from 2308318.0 units to 4711929.0 units. ABB expects delivery in 12.0 days to 23.0 days, pays $909.918 per unit, and settles invoices exactly 72.0 days later; if stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Bosch Automotive also purchases the drone, with requests coming in between 27.0 days and 55.0 days. Its demand is typically 4386038.0 units with variability of about 1249993.0 units, customer lead time falls between 8.0 days and 10.0 days, and the selling price is $3097.096 per unit. Bosch pays in 15.0 days to 54.0 days, and if inventory is unavailable, the sale is lost outright. The network is straightforward: all 3 suppliers connect into the 2 facilities, and product flow moves from the Production Center to the Regional Depot. The model runs for a 200-day horizon with 5 replications.

