arge for these in-process parts, with 1226822 units of frame_structure on hand and 2102751 units of circuit_board, and they carry holding costs of $7.399 per unit and $6.785 per unit respectively; frame_structure is reviewed every 1 hour, while circuit_board is also reviewed every 1 hour and has a shortage penalty of $18.613 per unit. Final assembly is concentrated at the Integration Center. One manufacturing flow there, called testing, turns aluminum_sheet, carbon_fiber, frame_structure, and circuit_board into medical_device, using the Injection Molder. That step takes anywhere from 13.0 hours to 27.0 hours. The Injection Molder itself has capacity for 2 units, a constant service time of 4.596 hours, runs with batching enabled in lots of 85 units, waits up to 13.406 hours to form a batch, and costs $1.983 per hour to operate. The other flow at the same Integration Center is assembly for camera, using frame_structure, circuit_board, carbon_fiber, and aluminum_sheet, with a fixed 28.0-hour cycle and no named resource attached. The bill of material for the medical_device is fairly heavy: 2 units of aluminum_sheet, 1 units of carbon_fiber, 4 units of frame_structure, and 2 units of circuit_board per unit produced. The camera requires 1 units of frame_structure, 4 units of circuit_board, 1 units of carbon_fiber, and 1 units of aluminum_sheet. Finished stock also starts high, with 1904705 units of medical_device and 4926448 units of camera in inventory. Medical_device holding cost is $1.632 per unit, shortage cost is $43.379 per unit, and stock is checked every 1 hour; camera holding cost is $2.628 per unit, shortage cost is $29.641 per unit, and it too is reviewed every 1 hour. Demand comes from three named customers. Broadcom Limited buys medical_device, placing orders every 30.0 hours to 35.0 hours, with demand centered around 4632330.0 units and varying by about 32006.0 units. Orders are delivered in exactly 11.0 hours, the selling price is $1013.055 per unit, and payment arrives 7.0 days after delivery. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the remaining demand is lost. Schneider Electric also buys medical_device, with arrivals between 22.0 hours and 31.0 hours, demand averaging 4713483.0 units with variability of 986084.0 units, customer lead time ranging from 10.0 hours to 28.0 hours, a selling price of $2703.252 per unit, and payment collected after 81.0 days. In that relationship, available units go out immediately and any remainder is backordered. Dell Technologies purchases camera with order arrivals averaging 23.0 hours, a fixed demand of 2858075.0 units, a 27.0-hour delivery commitment, and a sales price of $2008.25 per unit; Dell pays between 14.0 days and 74.0 days after receipt, and if camera inventory is unavailable, the sale is simply lost. Product flows from the Integration Center to the Warehouse for both medical_device and camera. The model runs in hours over a 730-hour horizon, with 5 replications.

