imes between 3.544 days and 8.34 days, batching enabled at 77 units with a maximum wait of 6.895 days, and failure behavior switched on: uptime averages 205.565 days and downtime averages 31.288 days. Running that resource costs $17.003 per day. Finished goods then flow from the Processing Unit to the Logistics Center, with both camera and CPU handled there. On the inventory side, camera starts with 1599580 units on hand, carries a holding cost of $2.386 per unit, a shortage cost of $18.309 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day. CPU begins with 4785758 units in stock, has a shortage penalty of $37.282 per unit, and is likewise reviewed every 1 day. Demand comes from two major customers. Schneider Electric buys camera, placing orders every 19.0 days with a fixed demand of 2308593.0 units each time. Customer lead time is 11.0 days, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $4450.919 per unit, and payment arrives 58.0 days after delivery. Siemens Digital purchases CPU with order arrivals averaging 14.0 days, demand fixed at 2458777.0 units, and delivery promised somewhere between 12.0 days and 17.0 days. For CPU orders, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered, the sales price is $2117.861 per unit, and customer payment comes in after 18.0 days. The modeled network explicitly links PrecisionParts, MegaSupply, and GlassCore Ltd. to the Processing Unit for inbound raw materials, and routes both camera and CPU from the Processing Unit to the Logistics Center. The scenario runs over a 365 days planning horizon with 1 replication.

