e cost of $4.726 per unit with review every 1 week. Supplier relationships are mixed. ElectroParts ships copper_wire in exactly 19.0 weeks at $133.038 per unit, offers net 29.0 days payment, and can supply up to 544805 units per order. PolymerSource is the second copper_wire source, delivering in 4.0 to 8.0 weeks at $282.197 per unit, with payment due 11.0 days after receipt and a limit of 514770 units. For aluminum_sheet, PrecisionParts has an exponentially distributed lead time with a mean of 28.0 weeks, charges $479.009 per unit, and is paid 57.0 days after delivery, while NanoMaterials Inc. delivers in 14.0 to 23.0 weeks at $285.062 per unit with 35.0-day payment terms. Plastic_pellet comes either from BatteryTech, which delivers in 1.0 to 12.0 weeks at $191.545 per unit, gets paid in 44.0 to 87.0 days, and can supply up to 570544 units, or from MegaSupply, whose lead time averages 5.0 weeks, price is $191.944 per unit, payment is due after 20.0 days, and capacity is 257764 units. The inbound links route all of these suppliers into the Processing Unit, and the BatteryTech lane specifically takes 6.0 weeks to transfer to the plant. Manufacturing is also split across the same Processing Unit. The testing step converts power_module, frame_structure, and plastic_pellet into camera in 8.0 to 9.0 weeks and requires the Packaging Machine. That Packaging Machine has capacity for 2 units at a time, runs with a fixed service time of 6.746 weeks, stays up for 419.515 weeks at a stretch, then goes down with downtime averaging 25.756 weeks, and costs $8.019 per week to operate. The cutting step turns plastic_pellet, copper_wire, and power_module into laptop in 6.0 to 11.0 weeks. The coating step transforms aluminum_sheet into FPGA in a consistent 17.0 weeks. The CNC Machine is another shop resource with capacity of 2 units, a fixed service time of 1.32 weeks, uptime of 84.42 weeks, downtime of exactly 22.56 weeks, and an operating cost of $13.853 per week. Finished goods then move from the Processing Unit to the Distribution Center for camera, laptop, and FPGA. Customer demand is substantial. Schneider Electric places camera orders every 28.0 weeks, with demand ranging from 2171163.0 units to 4254688.0 units per order, expects delivery in 29.0 weeks, pays at $2840.172 per unit, and settles invoices in 16.0 to 63.0 days; if stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Apple Supply buys laptop every 15.0 weeks in fixed orders of 3970531.0 units, accepts a 20.0-week customer lead time, pays $3983.535 per unit, and has 55.0-day payment terms; under shortage, whatever is available is shipped and the rest is lost. Lenovo Group purchases FPGA on a uniform 20.0-to-20.0-week cycle, with demand centered around 3966774.0 units and varying by about 1097084.0 units, receives orders in 25.0 weeks, pays $4112.112 per unit, and remits payment in 18.0 to 38.0 days; shortages are handled by shipping available stock and backordering the balance. The model runs in weeks over a 100-week horizon, with results collected after a 30-week warm-up period.

