3 units for FPGA. Tablet stock is checked every 1 day and costs $9.04 per unit to hold, while FPGA is also reviewed every 1 day, with holding cost of $0.877 per unit and shortage cost of $49.828 per unit. Production at Assembly Hub happens through 2 manufacturing steps listed under the same site name. The cutting operation converts epoxy_compound and copper_wire into tablet, and that step takes exactly 27.0 days per cycle. The molding operation turns steel_rod and sensor_array into FPGA, and that cycle is a much shorter 7.0 days. There is also a Robot Arm resource in the system with capacity for 4 units, a service time ranging from 4.724 days to 6.882 days, and an operating cost of $5.219 per day. On the demand side, TechCorp buys tablet with orders arriving every 22.0 days, each order fixed at 3830745.0 units. Customer lead time is 12.0 days, the selling price is $2737.878 per unit, and payment is received 9.0 days later. If stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Schneider Electric purchases FPGA with order arrivals following an exponential pattern averaging 59.0 days. Its order volume is normally centered on 2962298.0 units with variability of 285922.0 units, customer lead time is 16.0 days, the selling price is $14.085 per unit, and cash is collected 27.0 days after delivery. For shortages, the business ships what it has and loses the rest. In terms of physical flow, Memory Star, MetalFab Inc., and PolymerSource all feed the Assembly Hub, and both tablet and FPGA move onward from Assembly Hub to Fulfillment Hub. The model runs for 100 days total, with results taken after a 30-day warm-up period, across 10 replications.

