eel_rod for assembly, producing FPGA in a fixed 1.0-day cycle. Starting inventory is quite large: 457417 units of heat_sink, 516234 units of CPU, and 4369445 units of FPGA. CPU inventory is reviewed every 1 day and carries a shortage cost of $47.655 per unit, while FPGA inventory is also reviewed every 1 day and has a holding cost of $9.141 per unit. Demand comes from two major customers. Bosch Automotive buys CPU, placing orders every 16.0 days, with demand centered around 3028204.0 units and varying by 636841.0 units. CPU shipments to Bosch take 7.0 days, the selling price is $3053.853 per unit, and customer payments come in between 29.0 days and 39.0 days. If CPU stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Broadcom Limited purchases FPGA, with order arrivals consistently at 23.0 days and demand anywhere from 1955361.0 units to 3852210.0 units. Delivery to Broadcom takes between 2.0 days and 11.0 days, the item sells at $1461.909 per unit, and payment is received 54.0 days after delivery. For FPGA, unmet demand is lost if inventory is not available. The network links NanoMaterials Inc., MetalFab Inc., and PolymerSource into the Fab, and then sends both CPU and FPGA from the Fab to the Warehouse. The model runs for a 365-day horizon with 10 replications.

