This network revolves around a Manufacturing Site feeding a Logistics Center, with four suppliers upstream and three major customers downstream over a 500-day planning window with 1 replication. The raw inputs are gold_wire, silicon_wafer, and solder_paste, and the plant also builds two in-process items before final sale: lens_assembly and circuit_board. A lens_assembly consumes 4 units of solder_paste, 2 units of silicon_wafer, and 5 units of gold_wire, while each circuit_board uses 3 units of gold_wire. Finished output is split across three products: a GPU needs 1 unit of silicon_wafer and 3 units of solder_paste, a SoC requires 3 units of lens_assembly plus 3 units of gold_wire, 3 units of silicon_wafer, and 3 units of solder_paste, and a drone is assembled from 1 unit of circuit_board, 2 units of gold_wire, and 2 units of solder_paste. On the purchasing side, gold_wire follows a min-max approach, with replenishment triggered below 3813.03 units and raised to 10446.31 units; it carries a holding cost of $4.464 per unit and a shortage penalty of $34.852 per unit, with stock reviewed every 24 days. Silicon_wafer is managed similarly, with a reorder point of 4560.9 units and an order-up-to level of 14178.26 units, plus $3.192 per unit in holding cost, $2.661 per unit in shortage cost, and a 23-day review cycle. Solder_paste is also threshold-based, reordering below 4386.89 units and topping back up to 9298.75 units; its shortage cost is $31.46 per unit and it is reviewed every 20 days. Inside the plant, lens_assembly starts with 4427603 units on hand and is reviewed every 1 day with a holding cost of $7.493 per unit. Circuit_board begins at 3423701 units, with $7.77 per unit holding cost, $32.134 per unit shortage cost, and a 1-day review cadence. For saleable goods, GPU opens with 4868013 units, held at $7.111 per unit with a shortage cost of $20.228 per unit and reviewed every 1 day; SoC starts at 4709310 units with $7.449 per unit holding cost, $13.233 per unit shortage cost, and the same 1-day review; drone inventory begins at 1899959 units, costs $1.325 per unit to hold, incurs $7.879 per unit when short, and is likewise checked every 1 day. Supplier relationships are mixed. SiliconWorks provides gold_wire at $446.742 per unit, with lead times ranging from 11.0 days to 14.0 days, payment due between 36.0 days and 61.0 days after receipt, and a maximum delivery size of 979979 units; material from SiliconWorks takes 6.0 days to transfer to the Manufacturing Site. Process Go. is the second gold_wire source, charging $488.929 per unit, typically delivering in 13.0 days to 18.0 days, and offering payment terms between 32.0 days and 70.0 days. Memory Star supplies silicon_wafer at $414.017 per unit, with an exponential lead time averaging 11.0 days, payment due between 29.0 days and 38.0 days, and capacity capped at 428221 units per order. PrecisionParts handles solder_paste at $272.029 per unit, shipping in anywhere from 1.0 days to 9.0 days, with invoices paid in 40.0 days to 44.0 days and up to 913945 units available per delivery. Production is centralized at the Manufacturing Site. The inspection step converts silicon_wafer and solder_paste into GPU, with a cycle time anywhere between 1.0 days and 27.0 days and requiring Test Equipment. That Test Equipment has capacity for 1 unit at a time, an exponential service time averaging 9.977 days, and operating expense of $4.559 per day. SoC production runs through a fabrication step using lens_assembly, gold_wire, silicon_wafer, and solder_paste, and that process takes exactly 13.0 days. Drone output comes from a bonding step combining circuit_board, gold_wire, and solder_paste, with a cycle ranging from 12.0 days to 13.0 days. The Manufacturing Site manages solder_paste, GPU, and silicon_wafer in the inspection flow; gold_wire, silicon_wafer, solder_paste, SoC, and lens_assembly in fabrication; and drone, solder_paste, circuit_board, and gold_wire in bonding. Finished GPU, SoC, and drone units then move from the Manufacturing Site to the Logistics Center. On the demand side, Broadcom Limited buys GPU, placing orders every 39.0 days flat. Demand is centered around 690863.0 units with variability of 22456.0 units, customer delivery takes between 7.0 days and 14.0 days, and the business follows a partial shipment policy where available stock is sent and the remainder is lost. GPU sells at $620.413 per unit, and Broadcom Limited pays 50.0 days after delivery. Bosch Automotive purchases SoC every 32.0 days, in fixed lots of 4304429.0 units. Lead time to the customer runs from 13.0 days to 15.0 days, available units are shipped immediately and any shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $1250.22 per unit, and payment comes 81.0 days after delivery. Apple Supply orders drone every 44.0 days, with demand varying uniformly between 847697.0 units and 946577.0 units. Shipments to Apple Supply take exactly 6.0 days, shortages are partially fulfilled with the balance backordered, the drone sells for $1819.258 per unit, and customer payment arrives between 29.0 days and 61.0 days. Overall, the node structure links SiliconWorks, Memory Star, PrecisionParts, and Process Go. into the Manufacturing Site and then on to the Logistics Center, creating a fairly large-scale electronics and device operation with tight daily inventory reviews on in-house stock and much longer replenishment cycles for incoming raw materials.

