This setup revolves around making industrial_sensor units through a fairly simple two-site network: SiliconWorks, PrecisionParts, and Memory Star feed the Pilot Line, and finished industrial_sensor units then move on to the Regional Depot. Each industrial_sensor requires 3 units of microprocessor, 3 units of resin, and 4 units of glass_fiber, so the plant is blending electronic and material inputs into a finished industrial product. Microprocessor supply is sourced on demand from SiliconWorks, with lead times ranging from 17.0 days to 27.0 days, a purchase cost of $321.368 per unit, payment due between 29.0 days and 64.0 days after receipt, and a maximum order size of 288992 units. At the inventory level, microprocessor stock is reviewed every 21 days, carries a holding cost of $1.52 per unit, and a shortage cost of $0.256 per unit. Resin comes from PrecisionParts under a min-max style replenishment approach: when stock drops below 1454.89 units, it is brought back up to 4701.23 units. PrecisionParts delivers in exactly 11.0 days, charges $278.795 per unit, allows payment in 40.0 to 58.0 days, and can supply up to 650924 units per delivery. Resin inventory is checked every 6 days and held at $1.43 per unit, and the transfer from PrecisionParts to the Pilot Line also takes 11.0 days. Glass_fiber is also procured as needed, supplied by Memory Star with a fixed 24.0-day lead time, a unit cost of $317.194, net 45.0 days payment, and capacity of 555652 units per order; once shipped, it takes 7.0 days to reach the Pilot Line. Production happens at the Pilot Line in a testing operation that consumes microprocessor, resin, and glass_fiber and outputs industrial_sensor units. The cycle time for that operation varies between 10.0 days and 11.0 days. Although the operation itself does not list a required resource, the site also uses a Robot Arm with capacity for 5 units, a fixed service time of 2.141 days, and batching enabled at 58 units with a maximum wait of 11.458 days. The Robot Arm is not perfectly reliable either: it runs for an average of 362.631 days before failure and then stays down for exactly 39.369 days, while costing $14.291 per day to operate. On the finished goods side, industrial_sensor inventory starts at 1270107 units, is reviewed every 1 day, costs $9.078 per unit to hold, and incurs a shortage cost of $18.589 per unit. Demand comes from Siemens Digital, with order arrivals somewhere between 21.0 days and 51.0 days apart, and order size centered around 2849684.0 units with variability of 795134.0 units. Customer shipments are delivered in 9.0 to 17.0 days, sold at $3008.13 per unit, and paid 15.0 days after delivery. If inventory runs short, the business uses a partial fulfillment approach: available industrial_sensor units are shipped, and any unmet remainder is lost. The model runs for a 200-day horizon with 10 replications.

