.8 units. Lithium_cell also comes in periodic lots, averaging 44740.0 units with variability of about 5721.0 units, arriving every 22.0 weeks; shortage cost is $39.551 per unit and review happens every 6 weeks. Inside the network, Fab manages resin, gold_wire, SoC, and silicon_wafer and runs a bonding step that turns resin, silicon_wafer, and gold_wire into SoC in a fixed 3.0 weeks using the CNC Machine. That CNC Machine has a capacity of 5 units, a service time of 0.354 weeks, and operating expense of $5.813 per week. Plant A manages heat_sink, solar_panel, and sensor_array, and its fabrication process converts sensor_array and heat_sink into solar_panel in exactly 2.0 weeks. Manufacturing Site handles GPU, sensor_array, lithium_cell, heat_sink, and power_module, where assembly combines power_module, lithium_cell, sensor_array, and heat_sink into GPU over 29.0 weeks. Starting stocks are substantial: 2331893 units of power_module, 850964 units of sensor_array, 3616244 units of heat_sink, 2192676 units of SoC, 3531202 units of solar_panel, and 1254776 units of GPU. Inventory economics differ by item: power_module carries at $0.178 per unit with review every 1 week; sensor_array carries at $5.205 per unit, has a shortage penalty of $39.494 per unit, and is checked every 1 week; SoC carries at $1.599 per unit, shortage costs are $15.083 per unit, and review is every 1 week; solar_panel carries at $0.812 per unit with a 1-week review cycle. Customer flow is also product-specific. TechCorp orders SoC every 28.0 weeks for exactly 3873837.0 units, expects delivery in 5.0 to 10.0 weeks, pays $2827.466 per unit, and settles invoices 64.0 days later; if inventory is short, available units ship and the rest of the demand is lost. Schneider Electric buys solar_panel with order arrivals between 16.0 and 31.0 weeks, demand centered around 3356341.0 units with variability of 440562.0 units, customer lead time of 1.0 to 24.0 weeks, a selling price of $1761.022 per unit, and 89.0-day payment terms; unfilled demand is backordered. Sony Electronics purchases GPU somewhere between every 9.0 and 13.0 weeks, with demand ranging from 2486235.0 units to 3908897.0 units, a consistent 22.0-week customer lead time, a price of $929.144 per unit, and payment in 24.0 to 77.0 days; here too, the business ships what is available and loses the remainder. In terms of physical flow, all five suppliers—FastChip Corp., BatteryTech, GlassCore Ltd., Memory Star, and CarbonSupply Co.—feed the manufacturing side, and finished SoC, solar_panel, and GPU output moves from Fab, Plant A, and Manufacturing Site into the Storage Facility. The model runs over a 200-week horizon with 5 replications.

