 units, waits up to 14.06 weeks to form a batch, and incurs an operating cost of $1.655 per week. The molding operation at the same Production Center turns copper_wire, resin, and epoxy_compound into FPGA, and each run takes exactly 28.0 weeks. On the finished goods side, CPU starts with 362832 units in inventory, carries a holding cost of $7.174 per unit, a shortage cost of $21.4 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week. FPGA begins with 2211296 units on hand, with holding cost set at $9.546 per unit and review every 1 week. Customer demand is substantial. NXP Semiconductors orders CPU every 17.0 weeks, with demand normally averaging 912200.0 units and varying by 255850.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes between 2.0 weeks and 12.0 weeks, the selling price is $2220.057 per unit, customer payment arrives between 14.0 weeks and 31.0 weeks, and if inventory is short, available units are shipped while the remainder is lost. Sony Electronics buys FPGA every 21.0 weeks, with demand centered on 3995915.0 units and variability of 813376.0 units. Its delivery lead time runs from 17.0 weeks to 24.0 weeks, the selling price is $2735.121 per unit, payment is received exactly 54.0 weeks later, and any unmet demand is backordered rather than lost. In terms of network structure, all 3 suppliers—Process Go., PolymerSource, and ElectroParts—connect into the Production Center, and both CPU and FPGA then move from the Production Center to the Regional Depot. The model runs over a 500-week horizon, with results collected after a 30-week warm-up period and 1 replication.

