he Fulfillment Hub. At the Assembly Hub, the fabrication operation turns gold_wire into CPU in a fixed 27.0 weeks, with the site managing both CPU and gold_wire inventory before shipping finished CPU units to the Fulfillment Hub. Starting stock is very high for both finished products, with 748325 units of EV_battery and 421852 units of CPU on hand initially. Customer demand is also substantial. Samsung Electronics places EV_battery orders every 33.0 weeks, with demand centered around 1107378.0 units and varying by about 41286.0 units; customer delivery takes 16.0 weeks, the selling price is $68.267 per unit, and payment is received in 27.0 to 36.0 days. If EV_battery inventory is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Siemens Digital buys CPU every 12.0 weeks, requesting anywhere from 1478170.0 units to 1834671.0 units, with a customer lead time of 7.0 weeks. CPU sells at $1464.835 per unit, payment arrives 31.0 days after delivery, and any shortfall is handled by shipping what is available immediately and backordering the rest. The model runs for a 100-week horizon, with results taken after a 50-week warm-up period; 10 replications are included in the setup.

