 unit cost of $247.633, payment due after 23.0 days, and capacity capped at 608108 units; the move from ElectroParts to the Manufacturing Site takes between 2.0 days and 10.0 days. Inside the plant, all of aluminum_sheet, circuit_board, battery_pack, tablet, frame_structure, memory_chip, and ceramic_substrate are managed at the Manufacturing Site, where a welding step converts circuit_board, ceramic_substrate, frame_structure, memory_chip, aluminum_sheet, and battery_pack into tablet. That production cycle ranges from 3.0 days to 21.0 days and uses the Assembly Line resource. The Assembly Line has capacity for 2 units at a time, its processing time runs between 3.148 days and 4.243 days, and it costs $3.412 per day to operate. Starting stock is very high across the internal stages: 2747511 units of circuit_board, 3657541 units of battery_pack, 4311475 units of frame_structure, and 4528147 units of tablet. Inventory carrying and stockout economics at those stages are also defined: circuit_board has a holding cost of $9.264 per unit, a shortage cost of $33.884 per unit, and a 1-day review cycle; battery_pack runs at $8.608 per unit held, $25.205 per unit short, also reviewed every 1 day; frame_structure carries $7.529 per unit with a 1-day review cycle; and tablet inventory costs $8.355 per unit to hold, $19.677 per unit when short, again with review every 1 day. On the demand side, NXP Semiconductors buys tablet on a fixed 47.0-day ordering rhythm, requesting exactly 2886486.0 units each time. Customer delivery lead time is 6.0 days, the selling price is $2958.657 per unit, and payment comes in 26.0 days after delivery. If stock is tight, available units are shipped and the remainder of demand is lost rather than backordered. The model runs for a 500-day horizon, with 10 replications.

