t turns sensor_array, heat_sink, and plastic_pellet into tablet in between 5.0 and 8.0 weeks using the Robot Arm. That Robot Arm has capacity for 4 units at a time, works with an exponential service time averaging 0.943 weeks, batches jobs in lots of 31 units, waits up to 14.675 weeks to form a batch, and costs $18.508 per week to run. Pilot Line manages heat_sink, aluminum_sheet, drone, and plastic_pellet, and performs testing to convert aluminum_sheet, plastic_pellet, and heat_sink into drone over a 10.0 to 17.0 week cycle. The Conveyor Belt is also part of the system with capacity for 4 units, a service time ranging from 1.53 to 3.374 weeks, and an operating cost of $19.691 per week. The Logistics Center acts as the warehouse receiving tablet from Plant A and drone from Pilot Line. Material flow connects ChipCo, BatteryTech, MegaSupply, PrecisionParts, and GlobalComponents to Plant A, while Plant A sends tablet to the Logistics Center and Pilot Line sends drone there as well; the ChipCo to Plant A aluminum_sheet lane specifically takes 12.0 weeks to transfer. On the demand side, HP Enterprise buys tablet with order arrivals between 31.0 and 54.0 weeks, each order fixed at 1467434.0 units, customer delivery expected in 2.0 to 12.0 weeks, and any unmet demand is lost; the selling price is $1020.335 per unit and payment comes in 37.0 days. NXP Semiconductors purchases drone with arrivals between 27.0 and 44.0 weeks and constant demand of 730754.0 units, shipped in exactly 8.0 weeks; if stock is short, available units are shipped immediately and the rest is backordered. That drone business sells at $1102.79 per unit and is paid in 19.0 to 22.0 days. Schneider Electric also buys drone, ordering every 36.0 weeks for 473237.0 units at a time, with delivery in 12.0 weeks flat; this account follows a lost-sales approach, sells at $2916.401 per unit, and pays 45.0 days after receipt. Overall, the full network of 5 suppliers and 3 facilities is evaluated over a horizon of 3