This setup revolves around two semiconductor products, CPU and SoC, supported by three incoming materials—solder_paste, resin, and plastic_pellet—and a fabricated component called cooling_unit. Each cooling_unit is built from 4 units of plastic_pellet, 1 unit of solder_paste, and 1 unit of resin. The finished CPU then consumes 2 units of plastic_pellet, 3 units of resin, 1 unit of cooling_unit, and 1 unit of solder_paste, while the SoC is heavier on supporting parts, requiring 4 units of solder_paste, 3 units of cooling_unit, 1 unit of plastic_pellet, and 4 units of resin. On the inbound side, ElectroParts supplies solder_paste to Plant A with lead times ranging from 9.0 weeks to 12.0 weeks at $25.867 per unit, and payment is due exactly 67.0 days after delivery. That item is managed with a min-max style policy: when stock drops below 411.54 units, it is replenished up to 1324.55 units, with a shortage penalty of $35.581 per unit and inventory reviewed every 5 weeks. Resin comes from Memory Star on a demand-triggered basis, arriving anywhere from 2.0 weeks to 13.0 weeks, costing $235.003 per unit, with 39.0-day payment terms; its shortage cost is $46.982 per unit and it is reviewed every 27 weeks. Plastic_pellet is sourced from Process Go. as needed as well, with a tight 6.0 to 7.0 week lead-time window, a purchase cost of $345.755 per unit, and payment exactly 87.0 days after receipt; its shortage cost is $9.321 per unit and review happens every 13 weeks. Inside Plant A, cooling_unit stock starts at 1725982 units, is monitored every 1 week, and carries a shortage cost of $20.998 per unit. CPU inventory opens at 3600318 units, with a holding cost of $6.501 per unit, a shortage cost of $23.414 per unit, and a 1-week review cadence, while SoC begins with 2921464 units on hand. Plant A runs two separate manufacturing activities. In the inspection step, plastic_pellet, resin, cooling_unit, and solder_paste are combined into CPU, and the cycle time varies uniformly between 1.0 weeks and 6.0 weeks. In the polishing step, solder_paste, cooling_unit, plastic_pellet, and resin are processed into SoC, taking between 17.0 weeks and 20.0 weeks. A Robot Arm supports the operation with capacity for 4 units at a time, a service time between 3.609 weeks and 8.449 weeks, batch processing turned on at 100 units per batch, and a maximum wait of 9.179 weeks before a batch is released; it also costs $16.058 per week to run. Plant A manages cooling_unit, CPU, resin, solder_paste, and plastic_pellet in one manufacturing flow, and cooling_unit, solder_paste, resin, SoC, and plastic_pellet in the other, with finished CPU and SoC then moving from Plant A to the Storage Facility warehouse. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys CPU with orders arriving every 58.0 weeks, each for 2493276.0 units, and expects delivery in 2.0 to 6.0 weeks. CPU sells at $4329.032 per unit, customers pay 38.0 days after delivery, and if inventory is unavailable the sale is simply lost. Siemens Digital purchases SoC with order arrivals averaging 33.0 weeks, demand centered around 2064376.0 units with variability of 528109.0 units, and a fixed customer lead time of 27.0 weeks. SoC is sold at $4042.672 per unit, payment comes anywhere from 44.0 days to 88.0 days later, and any unmet demand is backordered rather than lost. The network itself is straightforward: ElectroParts, Memory Star, and Process Go. feed Plant A, while Plant A ships CPU and SoC onward to the Storage Facility. The model runs over a 100-week horizon with 10 replications.

