This network revolves around a Fab supported by a Logistics Center, with MegaSupply, SiliconWorks, CarbonSupply Co., MetalFab Inc., NanoMaterials Inc., Memory Star, and FastChip Corp. feeding the plant with glass_fiber, gold_wire, and ceramic_substrate. The bill of materials is fairly layered: each sensor_array uses 5 units of ceramic_substrate and 2 units of glass_fiber, while each motor_assembly consumes 1 unit of gold_wire. From there, a tablet is built from 4 units of gold_wire, 3 units of sensor_array, 1 unit of motor_assembly, and 4 units of glass_fiber; a router requires 1 unit of ceramic_substrate and 2 units of glass_fiber; and an FPGA needs 4 units of motor_assembly plus 2 units of gold_wire. On the inbound side, glass_fiber is managed with a min-max approach, triggering replenishment when stock drops below 1978.43 units and bringing it back up to 2533.34 units, with a holding cost of $3.624 per unit, a shortage penalty of $37.077 per unit, and a review every 22 hours. Gold_wire is sourced on demand, with a holding cost of $9.9 per unit and a review cadence of 7 hours. Ceramic_substrate arrives in periodic lots ranging from 7466.0 units to 15296.0 units, with deliveries scheduled anywhere from 12.0 hours to 22.0 hours apart; its shortage cost is $34.872 per unit and it is reviewed every 16 hours. The plant starts with 1785474 units of sensor_array on hand, where shortages cost $15.428 per unit and reviews happen every 1 hour, and 1984006 units of motor_assembly, held at $4.848 per unit with the same 1-hour review cycle. Finished stock opens at 1970820 units of tablet, 413055 units of router, and 3254010 units of FPGA. Supplier terms vary quite a bit. MegaSupply provides glass_fiber with a fixed 2.0-hour lead time at $415.912 per unit, and invoices are paid 54.0 hours after delivery; shipments from MegaSupply to the Fab take 11.0 hours. SiliconWorks supplies gold_wire with an exponential lead time averaging 5.0 hours, charges $412.953 per unit, offers 80.0-hour payment terms, and can supply up to 922009 units per order; transfer to the Fab takes between 7.0 hours and 12.0 hours. CarbonSupply Co. handles ceramic_substrate with a steady 23.0-hour lead time at $84.016 per unit, and payment falls somewhere between 26.0 hours and 63.0 hours after receipt. MetalFab Inc. is the alternate ceramic_substrate source, with an exponential lead time averaging 13.0 hours, a higher price of $283.899 per unit, 76.0-hour payment terms, and a maximum order size of 204663 units. NanoMaterials Inc. also sells glass_fiber, typically arriving in about 14.0 hours, at $366.375 per unit and with payment due after 24.0 hours. Memory Star supports gold_wire supply with an exponential lead time averaging 20.0 hours, a unit cost of $419.351, payment terms between 24.0 hours and 77.0 hours, and capacity capped at 629903 units. FastChip Corp. adds another glass_fiber lane, with lead times varying uniformly between 9.0 hours and 21.0 hours, a cost of $405.889 per unit, and payment terms ranging from 46.0 hours to 80.0 hours. Inside the Fab, tablet production runs through a molding step using gold_wire, sensor_array, motor_assembly, and glass_fiber as inputs, producing tablet output in a cycle that ranges from 16.0 hours to 24.0 hours. That operation depends on a Conveyor Belt with capacity for 1 unit at a time and a service time fixed at 0.157 hours. The belt is batched, so work moves in lots of 12 units with a maximum wait of 17.149 hours before release. It also experiences breakdown behavior: uptime is a consistent 366.901 hours, downtime is 35.357 hours, and operating expense runs at $2.615 per hour. Router production at the same Fab goes through inspection, converting ceramic_substrate and glass_fiber into router units in exactly 16.0 hours. FPGA output comes from a cutting step, where motor_assembly and gold_wire are processed into FPGA units in anywhere from 14.0 hours to 22.0 hours. Finished goods then move from the Fab to the Logistics Center for tablet, router, and FPGA distribution. On the customer side, TechCorp orders tablets every 47.0 hours, demanding exactly 3011601.0 units each time, with customer lead time fixed at 6.0 hours. Tablets sell at $1250.649 per unit, customers pay 40.0 hours after delivery, and if demand exceeds stock, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Sony Electronics buys routers every 43.0 hours, with order size ranging from 1565486.0 units to 2182030.0 units, and expects delivery in 8.0 to 27.0 hours. Router pricing is $1329.496 per unit, payment comes after 25.0 hours, and shortages follow the same partial-ship, lost-remainder approach. Bosch Automotive places FPGA orders every 10.0 hours for 4772240.0 units, with delivery promised in 15.0 to 25.0 hours. FPGA sells for $2741.537 per unit, payment arrives between 24.0 hours and 47.0 hours, and any shortfall is handled by shipping what is available immediately and backordering the rest. The model runs in hours over a 100-hour horizon with 1 replication.

