 lens_assembly and epoxy_compound into CPU in anywhere from 16.0 weeks to 28.0 weeks. The same Main Factory also manages router, gold_wire, carbon_fiber, and epoxy_compound for a molding step that converts those raw inputs into router in a 7.0-week to 22.0-week cycle. Starting stock is fairly large for the semi-finished side, with 4727503 units of lens_assembly on hand; lens_assembly carries a holding cost of $7.657 per unit, a shortage penalty of $37.844 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week. Finished goods begin with 1559167 units of CPU and 173817 units of router, and router inventory adds a holding cost of $8.749 per unit with review every 1 week. Product flow then moves from Main Factory to the Distribution Center for both CPU and router. Demand comes from two major customers. Siemens Digital orders CPU every 30.0 weeks, with demand roughly 2620269.0 units on average and variability of 516562.0 units. Customers wait if stock is unavailable, the quoted delivery commitment is 23.0 weeks, the selling price is $983.101 per unit, and payment is received 79.0 days later. NXP Semiconductors buys router every 56.0 weeks, with order volumes ranging from 2368159.0 units to 4463175.0 units. Router demand is also handled on a backorder basis, promised in 7.0 weeks, sold at $3921.98 per unit, and paid in 49.0 days. Structurally, the network links the three suppliers—Memory Star, FastChip Corp., and GlassCore Ltd.—to Main Factory, and from there routes CPU and router through the Distribution Center. The model runs in weeks over a 730-week horizon, with 5 replications.

