akes between 4.0 days and 6.0 days. Inside the plant, motor_assembly starts with 2388258 units on hand and has a holding cost of $1.07 per unit, reviewed every 1 day. CPU inventory begins at 1572861 units, with carrying cost of $3.188 per unit and daily review every 1 day, while camera starts at 2212427 units and is reviewed every 1 day with a shortage cost of $30.123 per unit. The Integration Center runs an assembly operation for CPU using silicon_wafer, plastic_pellet, motor_assembly, and carbon_fiber as inputs, producing CPU in a cycle that ranges from 15.0 days to 25.0 days. That step requires the Assembly Line, which has capacity for 4 units, an exponential service time with a mean of 4.363 days, and an operating cost of $1.98 per day. The same Integration Center also runs a molding process for camera, taking memory_chip and carbon_fiber as inputs and finishing each batch in a fixed 19.0 days, with no named resource attached. Finished CPUs and cameras are then sent from the Integration Center to the Regional Depot. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys CPU on a fixed schedule every 57.0 days, with order quantities somewhere between 2552493.0 units and 3751011.0 units. Customer shipments for CPU take exactly 6.0 days, the selling price is $1082.663 per unit, and if stock is short, available units ship immediately while the remainder is backordered; Apple Supply pays 64.0 days after delivery. Lenovo Group purchases camera with arrivals averaging 44.0 days, and demand is centered around 2854519.0 units with variability of 25185.0 units. Camera orders are delivered in 7.0 days at a selling price of $2884.423 per unit, using the same partial-fulfillment-with-backorder approach, and customer payment comes between 36.0 days and 79.0 days after delivery. Overall, the model runs for a 500-day horizon, with 5 replications.

